{{Administrative divisions of Guajicutea}}

The '''administrative divisions of the Republic of Guajicutea''' are divided into four nested layers: districtos, special ciudades, municipalidads, and barrios. The system combines inherited territorial boundaries with later urban administration. Districtos, municipalidads, and ciudades are all instruments of delegated government rather than separate sovereign units.

The central state retains the highest authority and grants administrative powers downward by law. Municipalidads manage ordinary local government where population and revenue are sufficient. Districtos act as a support layer for municipalities that are too small to provide every service on their own, and municipal councils may delegate selected functions to the district level when wider coordination is more efficient. Ciudades are pooled urban systems created by municipalidads within a metropolitan area, which authorize a city executive council to coordinate shared services across municipal boundaries.

== Powers and administration ==

The modern structure is a product of constitutional reform and later urban growth. The district system descends from the older feudal bikabá order, while municipalidads and barrios were formalized as practical divisions for settlement, land administration, and local government. Ciudades were added later, after the main district and municipal framework already existed, to manage the service needs of large urban concentrations.

== Districtos ==

Districtos are the top local layer below the national government. They provide the territorial frame for Assembly apportionment, regional coordination, and major infrastructure planning. Each district has a directly elected governor with a four-year term and a six-member district council, with three seats renewed every two years. The 17 current districtos include the 16 mainland districts and the separate island district of [[Warikú]].

Politically, districtos matter because they are the scale at which the state handles matters that cross municipal boundaries: road links, water basins, school siting, boundary disputes, and capital spending. District councils or district boards are made up of delegates from the municipalidads inside each district. They do not run everyday services, but they coordinate the larger questions that affect the district as a whole.

The district system is historically conservative. Its boundaries preserve the older feudal territorial logic, even though the old lordship structure was replaced. In modern use, district identity remains important in elections, patronage, and regional planning.

== Ciudades ==

Ciudades are special urban systems created where several municipalidads had grown into a single metropolitan region. They do not replace the municipalidads inside them. Instead, they sit above them and coordinate shared urban services. Each city executive council is composed of one delegate from each municipal council inside the city system, so the city level reflects the councils of the municipalities that make it up.

The first ciudad system was created in 1968 during the [[Waribá]] reorganization, when the capital area had grown large enough that multiple municipalidads needed joint administration. [[Barikú]] followed in 1972, and [[Tabakurí]] was added in 2002 with two surrounding municipalidads.

A ciudad is governed through a city executive council elected from the councils of the municipalidads within the city system. That council handles matters that are easier to manage across the whole urban area: transit, water, sanitation coordination, policing coordination, land-use planning, major roads, and capital budgets. The individual municipalidads still exist, but the ciudad gives the urban area a common administrative layer.

== Municipalidads ==

Municipalidads are the ordinary local government units of Guajicutea. They were drawn around existing towns, ports, suburbs, and nearby rural settlements so that administrative boundaries would match the way people already lived and moved. Each municipalidad has a six-member elected council, with three seats renewed every two years under the regular four-year cycle.

Each municipalidad is run by an elected municipal council. These councils handle local roads, waste collection, markets, registries, neighborhood services, permit approvals, and liaison with the district level. They are also where most local political competition takes place, because they sit closest to housing, land, and everyday service delivery.

In places that fall under a ciudad system, municipal councils also send delegates upward to the city executive council. That makes municipalidads both local governments in their own right and the building blocks of larger urban administration.

== Barrios ==

Barrios are the smallest formal divisions. They are neighborhood units within towns and cities, usually based on older settlement patterns, road networks, market areas, or parish boundaries.

Barrios do not normally function as full governments. Instead, they are represented through neighborhood committees, ward associations, or community boards. These bodies deal with small-scale civic matters such as street maintenance, local complaints, festivals, and mutual aid. Their role is informal but important, because barrio networks are often the first place residents organize around local issues.

== Historical development ==

The present system was consolidated through constitutional reform, which formalized districtos, municipalidads, and barrios as the main internal divisions of the state. The district layer kept the logic of the old bikabá territories, while municipalidads were formed by drawing reasonable boundaries around pre-existing settlements and urban centers. Barrios were formalized at the same time as the smallest local layer inside towns and cities.

Ciudades came later, once urban growth made the older municipal structure too fragmented for large metropolitan areas. Their creation reflected practical needs rather than territorial redesign. Waribá was the first case, followed by Barikú and Tabakurí as the urban footprint widened.

== Current structure ==

In current practice, the system works from top to bottom as a chain of scale rather than a single grid. Districtos provide the broad territorial frame, municipalidads handle everyday government, ciudades coordinate the largest urban concentrations, and barrios keep the neighborhood-level social geography visible.

That layered structure allows Guajicutea to preserve older boundaries while still adapting to modern urban administration.

